# Assistant team operator guide

Run commands as the dedicated runtime user. After entering that user’s shell, `~/team` supplies the user-systemd environment automatically.

## Quick start

1. Run `~/team setup --list` to inspect profiles, then `~/team setup` to select enabled profiles. Login is optional and runs only when you choose it.
2. Authenticate selected profiles using their local `./login` commands.
3. Create a project: `~/team create-project example`.
4. Edit `~/projects.example.config` to assign enabled profiles to roles. Edit `~/projects/example/assistant-prompt.md` for the goal, boundaries and role instructions.
5. Run `~/team start example`, then `~/team attach example orchestrator` and give the orchestrator a bounded task.
6. In another terminal, `~/team watch example` shows worker messages. Ctrl-C stops only the viewer.
7. `~/team status example`, `~/team stop example` and `~/team restart example` control that project only.

## Authentication and account isolation

Each directory contains its own installation, configuration, credentials, cache and history. Canonical commands remain `./claude`, `./gemini`, `./codex` and `./vibe`. Work/priv labels select isolated contexts; they do not invent provider capabilities.

| Profile directory | Native authentication |
| --- | --- |
| `~/claude-work` | Vertex ADC; guided project, location and enabled model ID |
| `~/claude-priv` | Claude account with a plan supporting Claude Code |
| `~/gemini-work` | Vertex ADC; guided project, location and enabled model ID |
| `~/gemini-priv` | Personal Google OAuth through Antigravity; displayed browser/code flow |
| `~/codex-work`, `~/codex-priv` | ChatGPT device login; isolated file credential stores |
| `~/mistral-work`, `~/mistral-priv` | Native Vibe setup/account login |

For example: `cd ~/gemini-priv` then `./login`. No `gcloud` is needed for personal Gemini. The supported native CLI is Antigravity (`./agy`). After personal Google sign-in, exit it to see the saved-auth result. Its normal SSH/container token fallback uses scoped files protected by 0700 directories and umask 077; this is not encryption at rest and does not protect against root/sudo. No desktop/keyring packages are required. Model access remains unverified and its agents-connector team adapter is pending; use Gemini work for roles. No client-ID bypass or paid fallback is configured. Work Vertex login requires the locally installed Google Cloud SDK: the operator enables that selected dependency in encrypted deployment settings. The wrapper fails clearly if it is unavailable. No API enablement, billing changes or model probes occur during deployment.

At session end, `./login` reports **Failed or cancelled** or **Credentials saved; model access unverified**. Interactive exit without new credential evidence is not treated as successful login. Native account status and saved JSON do not prove model entitlement. No model check runs automatically; this setup does not yet expose a universal opt-in model verifier.

`./login --clear` clears only that profile’s saved auth and matching stopped project-role copies, then exits. Installation and non-secret provider settings remain. Exit active local CLI sessions and stop projects using that profile first; otherwise clearing refuses before mutation. This clears local credentials, without promising remote token revocation.

Mistral profiles work as standalone native CLI installations. Vibe participation in Agents Connector is required future work; project assignment to Mistral currently fails with an actionable error.

## Configuration

There is one environment per Linux user. `~/environment.config` is strict JSON containing eight profile enablement flags. `~/team setup --list` distinguishes enabled, installed and locally saved credentials without printing identities or tokens. Disabling a profile preserves its files and does not stop running projects.

Each `~/projects.NAME.config` contains `version`, `project` and `roles`; for example the role map can contain `"challenger": "gemini-work"` and `"builder": "codex-work"`. This file is the authoritative assignment. Prompts contain prose, not competing editable account settings. Legacy `Account: priv/work` prompt directives migrate once into this config. An unresolved connector assignment must be selected explicitly; account boundaries never silently change because a profile is disabled.

Changing a role account requires stopping and starting that project. Separate per-profile role homes and sessions prevent previous-account credentials carrying over. Native provider approvals remain enabled. Codex uses workspace-write/on-request; observer tools are restricted and connector general tools disabled. Connector service credentials/plugins are not configured.

Project listing status describes the target and named components: `running`,
`starting`, `stopping`, `stopped`, `failed`, `partial` or `unknown`. `partial` means
components are active while the target is inactive; `unknown` means a systemd or
Podman query failed and includes a bus/access suggestion. `running` describes the
target, not individual pane health or verified model access. Listing starts no
service and reads no project database, prompt or authentication contents.

Project completion commands are `~/team archive NAME` and `~/team destroy NAME`.
Both first check the named target, broker, gateway and containers, stop them only
if running, and verify that no project writer remains. Stopped containers are
removed without deleting images or shared model account profiles. A failed stop
leaves project data and configuration in place; an inactive project needs no
native harness or login to complete either operation.

Archive moves the whole project—including SQLite history/WAL/SHM, artifacts,
prompts and role state—to `~/project-archive/NAME-ddmmyyhhmm`, with its former home-level
`projects.NAME.config` inside. Every archive uses the operation-start local time
(day, month, two-digit year, 24-hour hour/minute), saved in private timestamp metadata.
Same-minute destination collisions fail before stopping; no archive is overwritten.
An archive matching the current project name exactly also blocks shutdown: use
`~/team rename-project OLD NEW` before retrying (provided by the rename feature). Archive directories are private (0700), the assignment config is 0600,
and internal file modes and symlinks are preserved. Archives can include private
role-local authentication copies and must stay outside Git. Restore is not provided.
Destroy permanently deletes the project directory and its home-level config.
Enabled named service instances are disabled. Only their failed state is reset;
bounded nonloading unit-list checks verify that systemd forgets all four instances
before files are moved/deleted. If a reference prevents collection, files remain.
Shared systemd templates and other instances remain.
Do not start, restart or edit the same project concurrently with either operation.

## Project files and sessions

`~/team --help` shows every supported command, required parameters and a brief
explanation without reading authentication or starting services. Unknown commands
explicitly report `Invalid command`; missing required parameters are a separate error.
Use `create-project NAME`, not `create NAME`.

`~/team --list` (or `~/team list-projects`) lists initialized project names and runtime status using read-only service/container
metadata, without opening their prompts, databases or credentials. `~/team setup --list` lists account
profiles instead. Empty or unfinished projects and symlink paths are excluded.

- `~/projects/NAME/assistant-prompt.md`: project goal and eight editable role sections.
- `~/projects/NAME/workspace`: shared artifacts, mounted at `/workspace/shared`; seven working roles read/write, observer read-only.
- `~/projects/NAME/db.sqlite`: project-local stock broker history.
- `~/projects/NAME/profiles/ROLE/accounts/PROFILE`: isolated native role state and private instructions.

New project templates use `[Project]` and role markers such as `[Role: Investigator]`,
`[Role: Private Connector]` and `[Role: Corporate Connector]`. All `#` lines are
comments, including the initial example instructions, and are excluded from native
harness input. Add active text beneath the comments or remove the `# Example:` prefix.
Blank roles are allowed; examples do not become automatic fallback instructions.
Existing legacy `# Role: canonical-name` files retain their previous interpretation
and are not rewritten. Duplicate, missing or unknown roles are rejected before rendering.

`~/team apply-prompts NAME` renders each role’s own instructions. Changes apply on next native launch, not halfway through an active conversation. Never put credentials in prompts or shared artifacts. Coordinate ownership before editing the same file.

`~/team start NAME` checks enabled assignments and saved auth before starting any project service, then starts eight native tmux sessions without an initial task prompt. Native interactive trust/approval gates may still require attention. `~/team attach NAME ROLE` attaches the selected session; detach with Ctrl-B then D. Restart retains disk history but starts fresh processes; automatic native session resumption is not promised.

The per-project systemd target is `assistant-connector@NAME.target`. It is not enabled at boot. There is no original default group or all-project master target. Direct user-systemd use after switching users may require:

```sh
export XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/run/user/$(id -u)
export DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS=unix:path=$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/bus
systemctl --user status assistant-connector@NAME.target
```

## Private observer

Worker-visible broker registration contains only collaboration roles. The observer is outside that roster and has read-only shared/context mounts. Continuous automatic observation is not implemented. `~/team refresh-observer NAME` updates its snapshot; `~/team deliver-observer-report NAME` copies its report privately to the orchestrator. Worker-readable threads do not receive evaluator reports automatically. The orchestrator must preserve this boundary when communicating.

See `~/README.TLDR.md` for condensed commands. Keep both guides current after lasting configuration changes.

## Environment diagnostics and version

`~/environment-logs` holds protected bounded deployment and runtime metadata, including login outcomes and project lifecycle results. It excludes credentials, prompts, conversations, databases and raw CLI output. Deployment controller failures before connectivity retain a local protected spool; the user-operated runner transfers safe metadata when reachable.

`~/team diagnostics --export /private/environment-metadata.tar.gz` creates an explicit sanitized bundle for manual sharing. `~/team version` prints installed VERSION/build metadata. No automatic diagnostic replication or coding-agent access to a fresh authenticated deployment is configured.

## VM storage

Fresh Linux VM provisioning expands the root filesystem to use its configured cloned disk capacity (20 GiB by default). The existing running VM retains its already-expanded capacity. Runtime team commands do not resize disks. Existing project and authentication data are preserved.

For headless Vibe login, open its displayed sign-in link manually; the scoped browser handoff keeps native polling active. Wrapper-owned outcomes and next steps begin `[AC-Agents]:`.
# Vertex model identifier

Claude work login requires a full enabled Vertex model ID, for example
`claude-opus-5`; `Opus` and `Opus-5` are not full IDs. Availability depends on
your Google project and location. The login helper does not probe model access.

Human-readable runtime diagnostics are in `~/environment-logs/events.log`, alongside
`events.jsonl`. Follow them with `tail -f ~/environment-logs/events.log`. Both use
matching event IDs, protected permissions, and a 1 MiB rotation with three backups.
Failure guidance is fixed safe text, not raw native output; use the user journal
for detailed service diagnostics. Deployment transfers also provide protected
`deployment-<run-id>.log` alongside JSON, retaining up to 32 runs. Diagnostic exports
remain validated JSON-only; arbitrary text files are never exported.

The editable default template for new projects is `~/templates/assistant-prompts.md`.
Creation copies its role instructions into the new project; existing project prompts
remain independent. Deployment preserves operator edits and migrates the old home-level
template without overwriting an existing destination. Conflicting paths require operator review.

### Restore a retained archive

Run `~/team unarchive example-0910260800` using the archive directory name.
This copies its complete project state to the same dated project name and keeps
the original archive unchanged. The exact target is enabled but left stopped;
start it explicitly when ready. Structured configuration and generated role
instructions use the new identity; user prompts, artifacts, provider state and
conversation history are preserved. Broker sessions use the new instance name at
startup; stored agent identities and container-internal MCP paths remain valid.
Legacy plain archives gain their recorded archive timestamp, or directory mtime
when no metadata exists. Existing destinations, unsafe links, malformed archives,
and existing instances/containers refuse restoration before publication. Failed
restores roll back new files and enablement. Long dated names are checked against
the actual Unix socket path limit; use a shorter project base before archiving if
needed. No model calls or automatic service startup occur during restoration.

Before archiving a restored dated project again, use `rename-project` to choose a new logical name: the retained archive already has its exact current name.

`~/team rename-project OLD NEW` stops OLD when necessary, removes only its stopped
containers and forgets its old named systemd instances, then renames the project
directory and home assignment config. It updates structured project identity and
generated role instructions, preserving editable prompts, workspace artifacts,
conversation history and native authentication. The new target preserves OLD's
enablement and remains stopped; start it explicitly with `~/team start NEW`.
Existing new paths/configs, containers, loaded instances or excessive Unix socket
path lengths refuse the operation before shutdown. Partial rename failures roll
back directories, config and generated instructions where possible; an incomplete
rollback reports retained paths rather than overwriting them. Do not run lifecycle
commands or edit the same project's config concurrently.


Unarchive preserves existing relative links only when their targets stay inside
the retained project. It rejects other external, dangling or cyclic links. In
Codex native homes only, the regenerated `.codex/tmp/arg0` and
`.codex/app-server-control` scopes are omitted from the restored copy; the
archive remains unchanged. An exact daemon `current` pointer under `/home/agent`
is rewritten to a relative link only when it maps to the same native home's
retained release-cache directory. The staging tree is checked before publication
or target enablement. Credentials and other native state are preserved.

The exact Codex `.codex/app-server-daemon/daemon-updater.sock` is also omitted
from the restored copy when it is a socket; an ordinary file at that path is kept.

Project stop waits for the target, its three dependent services, containers and
IPC listeners to stop. Project start waits out pending shutdown; restart performs
a completed stop followed by start. A timeout reports failure rather than
claiming shutdown completed. Stop preserves unit enablement and project data.

For Gemini work on Vertex, enter the exact provider model ID. The current
Gemini 3.1 Pro identifier is `gemini-3.1-pro-preview`, including the suffix;
`gemini-3.1-pro` is not the published identifier. Confirm model availability for
your project/location in the [official Google model reference](https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/gemini/3-1-pro).
The login prompt checks identifier format, not remote model access. Keep the
selected ID in the local work profile's `.vertex.json`; work role launches copy
that configuration into the selected role home. Provider authentication files
remain outside Git.

Before handing over the first project task, attach each role with
`~/team attach NAME ROLE` and finish its native first-run screens. Review theme,
workspace trust, hook trust or requested tool permissions in that role's CLI.
These steps are separate from provider authentication; saved credentials and
login status do not prove actual provider requests succeed. Once the roles reach
their normal prompts, attach the orchestrator to give the project task. Manual
onboarding does not change the team's tool-approval policy.

## Personal Antigravity team role

Select `gemini-priv` for the challenger in `~/projects.PROJECT.config` before
starting that project. Native Antigravity uses its private saved login, scoped MCP
and native lifecycle hooks, with the existing container/tmux wake-up mechanism.
`gemini-work` continues to use Gemini CLI through Vertex. Do not change a running
role account; stop the project first. Approve only the intended native tool calls.

A fresh native role can ask for terms and interaction-data collection preferences.
Review those choices yourself; saved authentication does not authorize copying
onboarding flags or accepting new terms. Native access/wake acceptance is pending
until that confirmation is resolved.

`team status NAME` includes each role’s account profile (for example `gemini-priv`)
and configured model. These are configuration labels, not verified provider model
versions or entitlement. A missing explicit model is shown as `harness default
(unverified)`. Status does not run inference, inspect credentials or send terminal input.

`team watch NAME` colors sender/recipient role labels on interactive terminals;
message text stays plain, and available question correlation IDs remain visible.
Redirected output, `NO_COLOR` and `TERM=dumb` disable color. Restart only the watch
viewer to pick up formatter updates; the project team can keep running.

`team watch NAME` now prints the last 50 communication entries and exits.
`team watch NAME -20` prints the last 20 and exits. `team watch NAME -f` follows
only new entries; `team watch NAME -20 -f` prints the last 20 then follows.
Counts are 0–10000; either flag order works. This changes the earlier bare-watch
live-follow default. History is chronological by message ID, including asks and
replies once each. Follow subscribes before taking its history snapshot and skips
queued events already included in that snapshot. The existing upstream live
stream can drop notifications for an excessively slow viewer; it is not a durable
subscription. History reads only the project’s message table, with no model calls.

For a concise decision feed, run `tail -f ~/projects/NAME/workspace/DECISIONS.log`.
The summarizer appends timestamped DECIDED, DIRECTION or BLOCKED lines only after
meaningful accepted outcomes, agreed direction changes or blockers requiring
user input. The orchestrator requests these updates at milestones, without timers.
The summarizer is the sole intended writer; proposals, repetitive progress,
credentials, private connector content and hidden-role identities stay out.
This is an agent instruction policy, not a filesystem enforcement of append-only
writes: project workspace collaborators retain their existing write permissions.
Existing user prompts and log content are preserved during deployment. Already
running harness conversations need an explicit instruction refresh to adopt it;
updating files alone does not guarantee they re-read their role instructions.
